WEBVTT

00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:02.397
The year is 1977.

00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:05.753
You grow sympathetic neurons in a special dish.

00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:09.303
A barrier divides the dish into three chambers.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.691
Fluid barely crosses the barrier.

00:00:12.154 --> 00:00:14.974
Small clusters of cell bodies are in one
chamber.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.415
Their axons grow under the barrier into the next
chamber.

00:00:18.697 --> 00:00:24.671
So you can keep the fluid around the far ends of
the axons separate from the fluid around the
cell bodies.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.884
After the axons have crossed, you do two runs.

00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:32.120
1. You remove NGF from the far chamber only.

00:00:32.668 --> 00:00:34.494
NGF stays around the cell bodies.

00:00:35.499 --> 00:00:38.490
2. You remove NGF from the cell bodies only.

00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:41.334
NGF stays at the far ends of the axons.

00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:43.597
Answer three questions.

00:00:44.455 --> 00:00:47.939
1. Predict what happens to the axons and to the
neurons in the first run.

00:00:49.032 --> 00:00:52.233
2. Predict what happens to the axons and to the
neurons in the second run.

00:00:53.363 --> 00:00:58.011
3. What do the two results together tell you
about where a neuron receives its survival
signal?

00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:03.751
Robert Campenot built this dish and published
the results in 1977.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.025
The dish let Campenot ask where a neuron needs
NGF.

00:01:08.095 --> 00:01:10.148
- First run.

00:01:10.858 --> 00:01:15.687
When NGF was removed from the far chamber, the
axons there stopped growing.

00:01:16.293 --> 00:01:18.777
Later they appeared to degenerate (break down).

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:24.034
This happened even though the cell bodies behind
them were in NGF the whole time.

00:01:25.324 --> 00:01:25.832
- Second run.

00:01:26.712 --> 00:01:30.039
Removing NGF from the cell bodies had much less
effect.

00:01:30.669 --> 00:01:36.056
Many neurons survived, as long as their axons
reached into a chamber that still had NGF.

00:01:37.461 --> 00:01:41.456
The axons also did not grow into any chamber
that lacked NGF.

00:01:43.032 --> 00:01:47.072
So the part of the neuron that needs NGF is the
part at the target.

00:01:47.816 --> 00:01:53.848
An axon depends on the NGF in its own
surroundings, whatever the fluid around the rest
of the neuron contains.

00:01:55.106 --> 00:02:00.275
A cell body with no NGF of its own can survive
on the NGF that its axons collect.

00:02:01.828 --> 00:02:06.491
Whatever kept those cell bodies alive had to
travel the length of the axon to reach them.

00:02:07.356 --> 00:02:12.324
NGF could not have reached them through the
fluid, because the barrier kept the fluids of
the chambers apart.

00:02:13.373 --> 00:02:20.637
This is the founding result for what the field
calls retrograde signalling: signalling from the
axon terminal back to the cell body.
